Your response has a maximum of exactly 1 attained by exactly one observation, which is the signature of a response divided by its own observed maximum. Dividing by an extreme order statistic correlates every observation, discards the uncertainty in the divisor and biases ECx estimates upwards; it also forces one observation outside the open support of the Beta family. Prefer fitting the raw response and reading effective concentrations off the fitted curve with ecx(type = "absolute"), which is already the default. See Ritz et al. (2026) doi:10.1007/s10651-025-00698-y, and ?ecx for what to do instead.
Dropping the model(s) neclin, ecxlin as they are not valid in the case of a beta with identity link.
Your response contains 2 value(s) at 0, which cannot be fitted because a beta distribution cannot represent a zero. They have been shifted to 0.000292. The substitution is recorded on the fitted object; see ?bnec_record. If those zeros are meaningful -- for example individuals that died -- consider family = zero_inflated_beta() instead, which models them explicitly.
Your response contains 1 value(s) at 1, which cannot be fitted because a beta distribution is defined on the open interval (0, 1). They have been shifted to 0.999. The substitution is recorded on the fitted object; see ?bnec_record.
